# Aerospace ERP

Spring Boot microservices (13 services) + React frontend, running on Docker.

## Quick start (Docker)

Only Docker Desktop is needed (about 8 GB RAM free). No Java, Maven, Node or Postgres install required.

```powershell
git clone <repo-url>
cd Aerospace-ERP

# 1. Create .env and fill in the values (see "Environment variables" below)
Copy-Item .env.example .env        # Linux/Mac: cp .env.example .env

# 2. Build and start everything
docker compose up --build -d
```

The first build takes 10-20 minutes. After that, startup takes 3-5 minutes.
Open http://localhost:3000

### Login

| Field    | Value                                              |
|----------|----------------------------------------------------|
| Username | `admin`                                            |
| Password | the value of `ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` in `.env`  |

The admin user is created on first start only if `ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` is set.
Change the password after your first login.

## Daily commands

```powershell
docker compose up -d                  # start (Docker Desktop must be running)
docker compose down                   # stop (data is kept)
docker compose ps                     # list containers and their status
docker compose logs -f auth-service   # follow one service's logs
docker compose restart auth-service   # restart one service
docker compose up -d --build          # rebuild and restart after code changes
docker compose up -d --build finance-service   # rebuild a single service
docker compose down -v                # stop AND delete all database data (careful)
```

## Environment variables (.env)

| Variable                   | Purpose                                                           |
|----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `JWT_SECRET`               | Signs login tokens. Same value for every service (set once here). |
| `DB_PASSWORD`              | Postgres password. Docker uses it to create the database.         |
| `ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` | Password for the first `admin` user.                              |
| `MAIL_USERNAME`            | Gmail address used to send notification emails (optional).        |
| `MAIL_PASSWORD`            | Gmail App Password, not the normal login password (optional).     |

Generate a JWT secret:
```powershell
-join ((1..64) | ForEach-Object { "{0:x}" -f (Get-Random -Maximum 16) })   # PowerShell
openssl rand -hex 32                                                       # Linux/Mac
```
`.env` is gitignored. Never commit it. Copy it manually to each new machine.

## URLs and ports

| What                      | URL / port                                  |
|---------------------------|---------------------------------------------|
| Frontend                  | http://localhost:3000                       |
| API gateway               | http://localhost:8089                       |
| Eureka (service registry) | http://localhost:8761                       |
| Postgres (from the host)  | localhost:5433, user `postgres`             |

| Service                     | Port | Database                |
|-----------------------------|------|-------------------------|
| auth-service                | 8087 | `auth_db`               |
| inventory-service           | 8081 | `inventory_db`          |
| qa-service                  | 8082 | `qa_db`                 |
| notification-service        | 8083 | `notification_db`       |
| order-service               | 8084 | `workorder_db`          |
| production-service          | 8085 | `production_db`         |
| procurement-service         | 8086 | `procurement_db`        |
| reporting-service           | 8088 | `reporting_db`          |
| production-resource-service | 8090 | `production_resource_db`|
| sales-service               | 8091 | `sales_db`              |
| finance-service             | 8092 | `finance_db`            | 

The frontend calls each service's port directly from the browser, so this setup works
when the browser and Docker run on the same machine.

## Troubleshooting

```powershell
docker compose ps                          # a service that keeps restarting has a problem
docker compose logs --tail 100 <service>   # read its error
```

- **Login returns 500 or "connection refused" right after start:** services are still
  booting. Wait 3-5 minutes and check http://localhost:8761 until all services show UP.
- **Port already in use:** stop anything else using 3000, 8081-8092 or 8761, for example
  an old `start-all.ps1` run or `npm start`.
- **No admin user:** set `ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` in `.env`, then run
  `docker compose up -d auth-service`.
- **Start clean:** `docker compose down -v` then `docker compose up --build -d`.

## Running without Docker (development)

Needs Java 17, Maven, Node.js, PostgreSQL (port 5432) and Docker (for Kafka only).

```powershell
# 1. Create the 11 databases in your local Postgres (names are in the table above)
# 2. Start Kafka + Zookeeper
docker compose -f devops/docker/docker-compose.yml up -d

# 3. Start all 13 backend services (opens one window per service)
.\start-all.ps1
.\start-all.ps1 -Stop          # stop all Java processes

# 4. Start the frontend
cd frontend\web-app
npm install
npm start                      # http://localhost:3000
```

Do not run this and the Docker setup at the same time. The ports clash.

## Project layout

```
backend/           13 Spring Boot services + common-lib + Dockerfile
frontend/web-app/  React app + Dockerfile + nginx.conf
devops/docker/     init-databases.sh, Kafka-only compose for local dev
devops/vps/        production VPS scripts (systemd + nginx)
docker-compose.yml full stack for Docker
start-all.ps1      starts the backend without Docker
```
